he team's group ID.\",\"Use -DisplayName for the channel name and -Description for the description.\"],\"validation\":[{\"rule\":\"contains\",\"value\":\"New-MgTeamChannel\",\"hintOnFail\":\"Use the New-MgTeamChannel cmdlet.\"},{\"rule\":\"regex\",\"pattern\":\"-TeamId\\\\s+\\\\$teamId\",\"hintOnFail\":\"Pass the team ID with -TeamId $teamId.\"},{\"rule\":\"regex\",\"pattern\":\"-DisplayName\\\\s+[\\\"']?Announcements[\\\"']?\",\"hintOnFail\":\"Set -DisplayName to 'Announcements'.\"},{\"rule\":\"regex\",\"pattern\":\"-Description\\\\s+[\\\"']\",\"hintOnFail\":\"Add -Description with the channel description.\"}],\"solution\":\"New-MgTeamChannel -TeamId $teamId -DisplayName \\\"Announcements\\\" -Description \\\"Company-wide announcements\\\"\",\"explanation\":\"New-MgTeamChannel adds a channel to an existing team. You need the team's group ID (a GUID), which you can get from Get-MgGroup.\"},{\"id\":\"m3-l5-ex4\",\"type\":\"syntax-check\",\"prompt\":\"Write the command to filter groups by display name, finding the group named 'Engineering'. Use the -Filter parameter with an OData filter expression.\",\"hints\":[\"Use Get-MgGroup with the -Filter parameter.\",\"OData filter syntax uses: \\\"displayName eq 'value'\\\".\",\"The property name in the filter is displayName (camelCase).\"],\"validation\":[{\"rule\":\"contains\",\"value\":\"Get-MgGroup\",\"hintOnFail\":\"Use the Get-MgGroup cmdlet.\"},{\"rule\":\"contains\",\"value\":\"-Filter\",\"hintOnFail\":\"Use the -Filter parameter for server-side filtering.\"},{\"rule\":\"contains\",\"value\":\"displayName\",\"hintOnFail\":\"Filter on the displayName property (camelCase).\"},{\"rule\":\"contains\",\"value\":\"Engineering\",\"hintOnFail\":\"Filter for the value 'Engineering'.\"}],\"solution\":\"Get-MgGroup -Filter \\\"displayName eq 'Engineering'\\\"\",\"explanation\":\"The -Filter parameter uses OData query syntax to filter results server-side. This is more efficient than retrieving all groups and filtering with Where-